WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Okay, so we divided up the brain one way.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.870
Now we’re going to divide it up another
way, so this is the brain from the top down.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.599
You know you have two hemispheres and you
might think, “well why do you need two hemispheres?”

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:22.200
Do know that Gazzaniga, who’s a relatively
famous neuropsychologist, studied split-brain

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.200
patients.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.750
You know that your cortical hemispheres – your
brain is united down in the sub-cortical areas.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:35.420
It’s sort of stuck together, although you
have twins of many of the sub-cortical structures.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:41.050
You have two hippoc8ampi and two amygdala
for example – one on each side.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:46.850
At the top, the hemispheres are quite separate,
but they’re connected by the corpus callosum,

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:47.850
which makes them communicate.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:52.559
It’s kind of interesting because you wonder
why they aren’t all glued together.

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:57.870
What it seems to indicate is that there’s
some utility of functional independence, combined

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.340
with some utility of communication.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:04.239
So it might be like, “do you really want
to be networked at the same time to everyone

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:05.239
in the world?”

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:11.409
No you don’t, you want some narrow pathway
so that only the important information comes

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:12.409
through.

00:01:12.409 --> 00:01:16.030
So it’s sort of like, maybe the right hemisphere’s
the one kind of computational device, and

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:19.790
the left hemisphere is another kind of computational
device – of course they’re not computational

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:21.860
devices, but you get the point.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.000
Only the important information – the processed
information from the right hemisphere goes

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.000
across.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.270
And only the processed information from the
left hemisphere goes across.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.689
Something like that, whatever.

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:39.520
If you have someone who’s epileptic or that
has a brain illness of some sort, it’s usually

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:45.439
epilepsy but not always, and you do surgery
on them and cut the corpus callosum; what

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:50.830
you find, weirdly enough, is that each hemisphere
develops its own personality.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:56.650
This is other evidence, I would say, for the
idea that what the brain does is produce personalities.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:00.880
You can keep paring the brain down, and what
it’s going to do is produce lower and lower

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.880
resolution personalities all the way down
to the bottom – they’re just not as detailed.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.060
If you lost, say eighty percent of your cortex
randomly, you’d sort of being like a thumbnail

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.060
of yourself.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.180
So, you’d still be you, just not at the
same detail.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:23.510
Here’s an interesting idea from the Catholics,
it kind of goes along with the avatar idea.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:28.120
So the Catholics had been wrestling for a
long time with the question of how God could

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.129
have fit himself in a human body– because
you could imagine that could obsess Christians

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:38.060
– this great, big God in an itty-bitty body,
but God in there just bursts the boundaries.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.940
They have this idea called Kenosis, which
means emptying.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:47.209
The idea is that God had to empty himself
out before he could inhabit a human body,

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:49.830
and it’s very much like a thumbnail idea.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:55.310
Jung would have of course thought exactly,
that the self was a thumbnail of God.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:03:00.880
He wouldn’t put it that way, but definitely
that’s where his thinking was leading.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.650
So that’s a pretty damn cool idea.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:12.470
Anyways, so I developed this before I read
what I’m going to tell you, but it doesn’t

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:13.470
matter.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.980
I’m going to talk about it from another
theorist’s point of view.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.730
The guy I’m going to tell you about, his
name is Elkhonon Goldberg.

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:24.659
He’s a neuropsychologist, and he was one
of Luria’s students who ended up in New

00:03:24.659 --> 00:03:25.720
York.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:31.080
Goldberg was obviously influenced by Luria
and all the unknown, Grey, and all those people

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.299
that I’ve already talked to you about.

00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:35.450
He was interested in why you have two hemispheres.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:40.360
Well, there’s a bunch of things you can
say, broadly speaking about the hemispheres,

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:42.430
and I’ve written some of them down there.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:47.950
The right hemisphere – this is an idealized
representation of a relatively highly lateralized

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.330
right-handed male.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:55.180
The reason I’m saying that is men seem to
be more lateralized than women, and the right-handed

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:59.200
lateralization is the standard kind.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.540
The brain can be lateralized all sorts of
ways.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:06.200
What you might say is that – there’s a
bunch of ways of looking at it.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:12.400
One way you can look at it is that your cortex
gets colonized by underlying structures.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.880
Each underlying structure is likely to colonize
a particular area.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:25.229
For example, when you are born and you wake
up and you see, your eyes are going to colonize

00:04:25.229 --> 00:04:27.210
your visual cortex most likely.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.979
It’s sort of adapted for that.

00:04:28.979 --> 00:04:34.669
If you don’t have a visual cortex then your
eyes will colonize something else.

00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:41.760
So the brain has proclivities towards a certain
kind of structure, especially early in development,

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.520
it’s unbelievably plastic, and so brains
can be organized and lateralized in a bunch

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.910
of different ways.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:54.850
To simplify it, what you do is you kind of
take, well let’s take the most straight-forward

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:58.810
and typical brain and talk about it as if
it’s the canonical brain.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:04.030
I think what you can say is, regardless of
where these systems are localized, they exist.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:08.400
So it’s an oversimplification – whatever,
doesn’t matter.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.880
What you’ve got with regards to your cortices
is a quick and dirty cortex, that’s the

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.820
right hemisphere, and then you’ve got a
long-term detailed cortex and that’s the

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:17.820
left hemisphere.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.750
The left hemisphere is specialized for language.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:24.520
Language cuts things up into little, tiny,
bitty pieces.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:28.750
That’s really useful if you’re doing little,
tiny, bitty things.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:31.040
You should be – precision, right?

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.630
Pinpoint accuracy; but that takes a lot of
time and a lot of computation.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:38.370
Whereas the right hemisphere says, “yeah,
yeah, let’s get to the bloody point.”

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.479
So it captures the thing in an image.

00:05:41.479 --> 00:05:45.070
So like the monster I showed you from the
hippie cartoon, that’s a right hemisphere

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:46.070
thing.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:52.020
It’s an image that represents a class of
entities; which, of course, images do.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:06:00.490
There’s this woman named - can’t remember
- autistic woman.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:04.550
She’s developed paddle-handling facilities
all across –

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.680
“what’s her name?”
Student “Temple Grandin.”

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:08.449
Temple Grandin, yes a very cool person.

00:06:08.449 --> 00:06:13.320
Yes I saw Temple Grandin speak at the University
of Arizona at the Consciousness Conference,

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:16.569
and it was the funniest speech I’ve ever
seen in my life.

00:06:16.569 --> 00:06:18.990
Not because it wasn’t perfect – it was
bloody perfect.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:23.720
She’s a tough woman – she looks a like
a cowboy, and she spent a lot of time with

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:24.720
animals.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:29.330
She comes out on the stage in her cowboy outfit
and she’s unbelievably straightforward and

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.690
without nuance – she’s autistic, she’s
severely autistic.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:37.800
You know, her mother helped her a lot, she
developed this thing that she would go inside

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:42.300
to compress her and hold her – like a little
machine and that would comfort her.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:47.669
Anyways, she turned herself into quite the
person and she thinks, she thinks like an

00:06:47.669 --> 00:06:48.669
animal.

00:06:48.669 --> 00:06:53.120
This is why she can design cattle handling
facilities, which are often slaughter houses,

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:56.600
so that the cattle don’t get all freaked
out as they’re on their way to their eventual

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:57.660
demise.

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:01.550
So one of the things she does, is she walks
through where the cattle are going, and anything

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:05.000
that bothers her, she assumes will bother
a cow.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.180
So for example, if the cows are walking through
this spiral as they’re on their way to the

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:14.460
slaughter-house and there’s a coke can lying
on the ground, all the cows will stop and

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.440
look at the coke can – it’s anomalous.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:23.599
Now, she talked a lot about how she thought
and what she said was that she could not devise

00:07:23.599 --> 00:07:26.110
abstract representations.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:31.860
So she said, okay, imagine how a child draws
a house.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:32.860
What is in that child’s house?

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:39.600
There’s a triangle and a square on the left,
and then there’s another rectangle, and

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.950
another parallelogram, and there’s a door
and two windows, right?

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.740
Then there’s a chimney with smoke coming
out – which is quite cool, it’s like why

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:48.740
is that?

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:49.740
Where’s the smoke?

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.820
You don’t see that in modern houses, but
kids still do that.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:57.919
And then there’s a tree, and usually there’s
a sun, and then - house.

00:07:57.919 --> 00:08:00.530
You think, oh, isn’t that cute, a simple
drawing.

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:02.120
That drawing is not simple.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:05.270
You’ve never seen a house that looks like
that in your life.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.240
It looks nothing like a house.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:10.640
It’s not a house, it’s not a picture,
it’s a hieroglyph.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.009
It’s a pre-linguistic representation.

00:08:13.009 --> 00:08:16.720
It’s like the essential elements of “house.”

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:21.270
So you might say what the child has done is
extracted out all those things that are common

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.410
across all houses, and then represented that.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:25.960
It’s not a drawing.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.081
Now if you take autistic people, sometimes,
and you get them to draw, they’ll start

00:08:30.081 --> 00:08:35.599
in one corner of the house, and what they’ll
draw is that house – with every damn brick

00:08:35.599 --> 00:08:36.860
in place.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:41.409
So there are autistic people, for example,
who – they can look at a building and they

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:43.580
can count every single window at one glance.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:48.300
Then, there’s another guy in England who’s
really famous, and this guy is unbelievable.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:53.360
You take him up in a helicopter and show him
a city that he’s never seen, then he goes

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:57.350
home and has a piece of paper, like as big
as a wall, and he starts in one corner and

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.170
he draws the entire city.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:03.620
He doesn’t sketch it out – it’s from
the bottom up.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:06.200
That’s autistic savant drawing.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:09.440
Often what happens is, it’s very realistic
looking.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:14.480
What happens if the autistic drawings of blank
language is they stop being able to draw.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:19.510
That’s because the abstraction interferes
with the realism.

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:22.930
It’s really hard to draw a hand.

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:26.890
The reason for that is if you draw a hand,
you draw a hieroglyph of hand.

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:28.700
This is what a hand looks like.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:31.529
You try to draw your hand and it’s really
strange.

00:09:31.529 --> 00:09:35.550
One of the really strange things about trying
to draw your hand is that in order to draw

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.560
it, you have to stop seeing it as if it’s
a hand.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.470
Then it pops out and you think, my God, that’s
the weirdest looking thing.

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:46.600
It’s like a tree root, you know, it’s
like a claw.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:50.070
You know if you look at your hand like that,
it looks nothing like a hand.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:52.300
That’s how a hand looks.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.300
What the hell’s that?

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:58.580
It’s like some sort of spider, but you have
to see it that way before you can draw it.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:05.279
So partly what happens is, is that your capacity
for abstraction interferes with your ability

00:10:05.279 --> 00:10:09.470
to see detail, that’s probably good – it’s
one of the ways that you simplify the world.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:15.390
Anyways, the right hemisphere looks like it’s
specialized for anomaly.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:19.620
The way I look at it is this: Okay, so you’ve
got your hippocampus down there, and it’s

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:23.880
keeping track of the comparison between what
you want and what you think is happening.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:24.880
“Bang!”

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:26.010
Something emerges, and it’s anomalous.

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:27.720
You don’t know what the hell’s going on.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.250
So you freeze, that’s the notoric representation.

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:33.190
Then you start to feel anxious.

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:37.500
Then the right hemisphere comes up with some
pictures – what might this be?

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:40.320
You can experience this at home, for your
own enjoyment.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:44.450
Let’s say you’re home at two in the morning,
it’s dark and you’re alone.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:47.380
Maybe you’ve watched a horror movie, so
you’re all nicely blank.

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:51.360
You know and then you hear a noise in another
room and that noise shouldn’t be there.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:53.649
So you’re going to go investigate, right?

00:10:53.649 --> 00:10:56.580
So let’s say the door’s just a little
bit open, and it’s dark in the room, and

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:59.810
you have to put your hand in to turn on the
light.

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:04.940
You just watch what your imagination populates
that room with when you do that.

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:08.120
You know, it’s like – guy with axe, snake,
alligator, like whatever!

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:14.760
You’re right hemisphere is going: there
may be one of this class of dangerous thing

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:15.760
in there.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:19.420
What it’s doing is it’s basically coming
up with a hypothesis: what sort of monster

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:20.420
might be in there.

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:25.420
You’re all freaked out about that, even
though you know – who knows what happened;

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:29.751
nothing but something creaked, but you’re
all on edge because your brain doesn’t work

00:11:29.751 --> 00:11:31.810
at night like it does during the day.

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:37.190
Everyone knows that – wake up in the middle
of the night, a manner of horrors go through

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:39.990
your imagination, and that usually happens
when you’re asleep.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:44.550
You’re asleep for that most of the time,
but sometimes you wake up and you worry about

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:45.550
everything.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:49.990
Anyways, so that’s the right hemisphere
– it’s coming up with pictures of what

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:51.260
might be there.

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:55.839
They’re generally monstrous pictures and
the reason for that is, well it’s obvious,

00:11:55.839 --> 00:11:57.880
what the reason for that is.

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:01.480
You’ll probably turn the light on and look
anyways, even though you might sort of peer

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:04.220
around the corner, and then when there’s
nothing you’ll be embarrassed about how

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.010
foolish you are.

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:10.610
But you can see the instinctual behavior right
there, and it’s wise.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:15.190
You know, there’s a low probability that
something terrible is there.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:21.279
Low probability times infinitely equals large
danger.

00:12:21.279 --> 00:12:26.279
So if there’s a two percent chance that
there’s a killer in there, it pays to be

00:12:26.279 --> 00:12:29.320
a little on the tense side, because being
killed really sucks.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:35.190
So you’re primed to feel negative emotion
more intensely, because you’re so vulnerable.

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:40.030
Anyways, that’s kind of what the right hemisphere
is up to – it does quick and dirty representations.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:46.670
It also does categorization, so it’s not
– it doesn’t give you exactly realistic

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:48.339
representations of what’s there.

00:12:48.339 --> 00:12:52.170
What it does instead, is it gives you categorical
representations.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:55.720
So you might say, “how should you represent
an unknown danger?”

00:12:55.720 --> 00:13:01.560
You should use an amalgam of all known dangers
to represent it.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.829
That is what it is in a sense, you know?

00:13:03.829 --> 00:13:08.230
That’s why I talked to you about the category
of “all dangerous things.”

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:15.250
So the Aboriginals in Australia have this
category which is, “women, fire, and dangerous

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:16.250
things.”

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:20.070
Well you think, well why are all of those
things in that category?

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:23.670
It’s not that hard to figure out if you
think about it, but it’s also a category

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:27.000
that you very frequently see, say in Disney
cartoons.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:31.630
So the Wicked Queen, for example, in Sleeping
Beauty – she’s constantly bursting into

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:35.610
flames and turning into a dragon and no one
seems to object to that – it makes perfect

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:38.529
sense that the Wicked Queen would turn into
a fire-breathing dragon.

00:13:38.529 --> 00:13:44.829
It doesn’t make any sense at all, although
it does, but it’s not a rational transformation.

00:13:44.829 --> 00:13:49.480
But because it’s in keeping with an archetypal
structure, everybody goes, “yeah, of course

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:50.480
that’s what she’s going to do.”

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:55.620
It happens all of the time in Disney movies
– like Ursula, remember her from the Little

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:56.620
Mermaid?

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:58.770
She’s trying to keep her daughter from becoming
conscious.

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:01.570
Yeah, nice Mother doing that.

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:06.570
She traps old Zeus – she squeezes his soul
to death and traps him in the underworld.

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:10.770
He’s also – well, we won’t get into
that.

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:14.000
Anyways, what happens to Ursula is when you
finally get her irritated enough she grows

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:21.220
into this huge, ship-destroying, storm monster
that’s part octopus – a giant octopus.

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:23.850
You think, “well, that makes sense.”

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:28.540
Okay, it doesn’t make a lot of sense, but
it’s a kind of hypothesis.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:33.329
Now that’s a representation of Mother Nature
– red and tooth and claw, and there’s

00:14:33.329 --> 00:14:37.269
also a reason that’s female – it’s not
female, it’s feminine – it’s actually

00:14:37.269 --> 00:14:38.610
a different thing.

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:40.560
We’ll get to that.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:45.649
So the right hemisphere, well it’s specialized
for anomaly – it does quick and dirt analysis

00:14:45.649 --> 00:14:47.580
under the influence of the limbic system.

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:52.029
It’s not linguistic, so I would say that
it’s still dominated by the sub-cortical

00:14:52.029 --> 00:14:53.029
systems.

00:14:53.029 --> 00:14:57.320
My suspicion is that animals are still dominated
by the sub-cortical systems.

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:59.510
Their brains work from the bottom up.

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:01.399
We have a top down module.

00:15:01.399 --> 00:15:04.720
That’s at least in part of the left hemisphere
linguistic system.

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:09.660
It’s a new thing that people have, and it’s
based on our capacity to abstract, so that

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:14.680
would partly be visual, like hieroglyphic,
and then also to transform things into linguistic

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:15.680
form.

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:19.750
It seems to me that that’s the part that
we really identify as ourselves, right?

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:24.290
It’s weird – let’s say you’re home
alone, and you get freaked out and all these

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:29.779
images come to mind; do you think of those
images as part of you?

00:15:29.779 --> 00:15:32.470
You think of them more as these things that
appeared to you.

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:35.310
That’s what you think about your dreams.

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:38.269
They’re things that appeared to you - they’re
not you.

00:15:38.269 --> 00:15:39.500
Why would you think that?

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:45.880
I mean, they’re in your experiential space,
so why is there that separation between you

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:46.880
and your dream?

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:48.040
“I had a dream.”

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:49.740
Not, “I thought up a dream.”

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:53.380
Or, “look what I created,” it’s like
there you are, the observer, and up comes

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:54.380
a dream.

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:55.740
It’s very, very weird.

00:15:55.740 --> 00:16:01.220
It shows you that there are forces operating
in your psyche that are impersonal, genuinely

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:02.220
impersonal.

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:04.779
That’s part of what you call the collective
unconscious.

00:16:04.779 --> 00:16:08.570
It’s the thing that generates these weird
images and protects you.

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:10.790
It’s nature, that’s for sure.

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:13.380
It can be absolutely terrifying.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:18.570
People can have dreams that are so terrible
– well like the one my little nephew had,

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:25.519
that’s pretty damn nasty; little greasy
dwarf things with big beaks, that’s a very

00:16:25.519 --> 00:16:29.509
ugly representation, and if that wasn’t
bad enough there had to be a fire-breathing

00:16:29.509 --> 00:16:31.959
dragon behind them, creating them endlessly.

00:16:31.959 --> 00:16:34.550
There’s no bloody-wonder he was waking up
screaming.

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:38.029
It’s like, “Oh no, I figured out what
life is like.”

00:16:38.029 --> 00:16:40.820
At four, that’s pretty traumatic.

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:43.250
So, okay, now the left hemisphere.

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:44.759
This is Elkonon Goldberg’s hypothesis.

00:16:44.759 --> 00:16:53.649
You get one hemisphere or system that operates
when you know what you’re doing and when

00:16:53.649 --> 00:16:55.540
what you want is happening.

00:16:55.540 --> 00:17:01.360
That’s the happy-talky part that thinks
it knows what-the-hell is going on.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:04.559
Then you have another part that only turns
on when you don’t know what’s going on.

00:17:04.559 --> 00:17:07.030
It’s full of anxiety and negative emotion.

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:10.750
The right hemisphere, by the way, is specialized
for negative emotion.

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:16.319
If you sustain right, pre-frontal damage,
you become inappropriately happy.

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:18.709
You’re full of positive illusions.

00:17:18.709 --> 00:17:20.809
You think, “well, that’s an excellent
thing.”

00:17:20.809 --> 00:17:26.380
It’s not – you make really impulsive risky
decisions and your life goes directly to hell.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:29.500
You need the negative emotion, obviously.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:33.929
The reason you suffer with negative emotion
is because if you didn’t suffer with negative

00:17:33.929 --> 00:17:37.820
emotion, you’d suffer from being destroyed.

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:39.419
Those are your options.

00:17:39.419 --> 00:17:43.220
There are people, by the way, who are born
without pain.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:44.820
You think, “hey, that’s a good deal.”

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:49.299
They never last past twenty, and part of the
reason for that is – you don’t notice

00:17:49.299 --> 00:17:52.720
this – but while you’re sitting there,
you’re going like this all the time.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:58.570
The reason for that is that if you sit for
any length of time on one part of your body,

00:17:58.570 --> 00:18:01.580
then it doesn’t get much blood and it doesn’t
get oxygenated.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:05.300
You do that, it bitches a little bit, a little
pain, so you shift.

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:08.500
You shift and you shift, so you’re just
doing this all of the time.

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:12.040
Otherwise you wear out your damn body and
you get bed sores, or that sort of thing.

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:17.080
These people that don’t have any pain, they
just wear themselves out.

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:20.060
So you need the negative emotion, it keeps
you going.

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:24.900
It can get out of hand and have its own pathologies,
but it’s definitely necessary.

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:27.530
So the right hemisphere does negative emotion.

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:31.130
Inhibition of behavior – that’s the freezing
that’s attended on anxiety.

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:35.650
It does image processing, holistic thinking
– so it’s the quick and dirty hemisphere

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:39.910
- pattern recognition, pattern generation
and gross motor action.

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:44.360
That’s interesting too, because the gross
motor action is, again, the quick and dirty

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:45.480
stuff.

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:51.299
If you need to slap something away, it doesn’t
really matter that it’s a gross-motor gesture.

00:18:51.299 --> 00:18:56.760
You don’t have to go like this, and be precise;
it can just be fast and sloppy, and it will

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:57.760
still work.

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:02.000
Now the left hemisphere is specialized for
when you know what-the-hell’s going on and

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:04.460
you can concentrate on the details.

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:08.430
So then you can think about it in terms of
the hierarchy that we discussed, right?

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:14.240
So what it implies is that at the level of
specific, fine, detailed motor output, the

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:15.810
left hemisphere is operating.

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:19.850
That’s kind of associated with positive
affect – I know exactly what I’m doing

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:23.270
and I’m making minor adjustments and everything’s
going well.

00:19:23.270 --> 00:19:29.120
If you go up the hierarchy towards more and
more abstracted representations then increasingly,

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:32.080
that’s moving over to right hemisphere space.

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:38.440
So that’s also partly why, when you disrupt
those higher-order structures, literally all

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:39.750
hell breaks loose.

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:41.419
So, left hemispheres.

00:19:41.419 --> 00:19:46.299
Operation in explored territory – positive
emotion, activation of behavior, so that’s

00:19:46.299 --> 00:19:47.350
also exploration.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:55.020
Word processing, linear thinking, detail recognition,
detail generation, and fine motor action.

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:58.390
So you need one system that works when you
don’t know what’s going on and you need

00:19:58.390 --> 00:20:01.620
another system that works when you do.

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:03.919
Sometimes you’re where you don’t know
what you’re doing and sometimes you’re

00:20:03.919 --> 00:20:05.669
where do know what you’re doing.

00:20:05.669 --> 00:20:11.740
Now Ramachandran, who’s a fairly famous
neurologist, who operates out of LA, he associated

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:16.430
the totalitarian ego with hyper domination
of the left hemisphere.

00:20:16.430 --> 00:20:19.980
The reason he did that is very cool.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:27.590
So he’s got these patients that have – what’s
it called, is it agnosia?

00:20:27.590 --> 00:20:30.470
It isn’t, we’ll call it neglect.

00:20:30.470 --> 00:20:32.330
Okay so this is what happens.

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:38.039
You have a stroke and the stroke affects the
back part of the right hemisphere, alright?

00:20:38.039 --> 00:20:40.640
So what happens?

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:44.660
Your left side is paralyzed; you can’t do
anything with it.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:49.470
Okay, so, however you also don’t notice
that it’s not working, so one of the things

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:53.960
you might do if you do become aware – you’re
laying in bed, you’ve had a stroke and for

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:57.420
some reason you notice that there’s an arm
lying in there with you.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:00.100
Then what you do is you try to pick it up
with your other hand and you try to throw

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:02.410
it out of the bed.

00:21:02.410 --> 00:21:04.700
When you find out that it’s attached to
you, because it’s not your arm, you’re

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:08.100
not very happy – or maybe you throw yourself
right out of bed.

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:12.250
Then if someone gets you to draw a clock you
draw half a clock and if you eat a plateful

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:16.820
of food you eat half the plate, unless someone
turns it – and then you eat half of that.

00:21:16.820 --> 00:21:21.760
So half the world is gone – it’s hard
to imagine what that would be like.

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:26.930
I think what it’s sort of like is – you
know how there’s a whole world behind you?

00:21:26.930 --> 00:21:32.049
You don’t detect it as not there – it’s
not black or anything, like when your eyes

00:21:32.049 --> 00:21:34.250
are closed – it’s just not there.

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:39.679
I think what happens with people with neglect
is that the “not there” becomes three

00:21:39.679 --> 00:21:41.630
quarters instead of just half.

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:45.429
It’s something like that anyways.

00:21:45.429 --> 00:21:49.590
You don’t know what’s going on and there
you are like this and someone says to you,

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:52.450
“I notice that you can’t move your left
arm today.”

00:21:52.450 --> 00:21:53.450
What do you say?

00:21:53.450 --> 00:21:55.990
You say, “that’s because I’m tired.”

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:59.930
Or, “I don’t feel like moving it.”

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:03.780
And so forth, so you have this reason that
you’re not going to move it.

00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:07.830
You might say the same thing about the left
foot, “yeah well I don’t feel like moving

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:08.830
it.

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:11.130
“ You’ll have some story about why you’re
not going to move it.

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:12.970
Okay so that’s fine.

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:16.230
Freudian’s thought about that is traumatic
denial.

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:21.940
It was so overwhelming to you that you lost
the left part of your body.

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:24.540
You couldn’t face it and you just denied
it happened.

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:31.299
It’s not a bad theory, it’s not quite
right, but it’s not a bad theory.

00:22:31.299 --> 00:22:33.539
I’ll tell you a couple of related stories.

00:22:33.539 --> 00:22:36.050
Let’s say you’re on dialysis.

00:22:36.050 --> 00:22:37.320
That sucks.

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:40.820
You have to have this big thing in your arm,
it’s like a shunt, and you have to go get

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:44.230
hooked up to a dialysis machine like all the
time.

00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:46.929
Your kidneys are failing and who wants that?

00:22:46.929 --> 00:22:51.320
Nobody, so everybody thinks: time for a kidney
transplant, and then they’re on a long waiting

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:53.170
list and they get one kidney.

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:56.220
That’s not very many kidneys.

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:59.789
Then they say, “okay, you got your kidney
and everyone’s thrilled about that, here’s

00:22:59.789 --> 00:23:02.800
your anti-rejection medication, make sure
you take it.

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:06.870
Then a year later you lose your kidney, why?

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:10.191
You don’t take your damn anti-rejection
medication, that’s the most common reason.

00:23:10.191 --> 00:23:13.890
You might think, well why in the world would
you not take your anti-rejection medication?

00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:15.909
The Freudians would say denial.

00:23:15.909 --> 00:23:22.980
Here’s a different hypothesis: Your whole
map of you is person with kidney.

00:23:22.980 --> 00:23:26.210
Then you lose your kidneys and it’s like
you’re a whole, weird thing that you have

00:23:26.210 --> 00:23:27.450
no idea about.

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:30.980
It’s virtually impossible for you to update
your model.

00:23:30.980 --> 00:23:33.190
You think, does that make any sense?

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:34.840
Have you ever lost a tooth?

00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:35.840
Right.

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:37.870
What does your tongue do for six months?

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:43.200
You’re sitting there and your tongue is
fiddling away there like a mad dog investigating

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:48.460
every teensy nook and cranny like it’s an
obsessive exploratory device.

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:50.600
It does it even when you don’t want it to.

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:53.320
Then six months later you get your mouth remapped.

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:58.220
That’s just a tooth, man; lose a kidney
and see what happens.

00:23:58.220 --> 00:24:01.570
Or lose the whole left side of you body, it’s
like, what are you going to do about that?

00:24:01.570 --> 00:24:07.039
Well, okay, then Ramachandran, he’s testing
these people for balance.

00:24:07.039 --> 00:24:14.840
What he does is he checks out this phenomena
called nystagmus, and so if you pour cold

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:19.190
water in someone’s ear their eyes will move
back and forth because it screws up the – you

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:23.590
know you have a balance system in you, in
ear your ear – and if you pour cold water

00:24:23.590 --> 00:24:26.950
in there it screws it up and then your eyes
move back and forth.

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:28.770
You know, it’s not very pleasant.

00:24:28.770 --> 00:24:34.250
What he found was that if he poured cold water
in the left ear, then all of a sudden the

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:38.029
people would wake up and go, “Oh my God,
I don’t have the left side of my body,”

00:24:38.029 --> 00:24:42.200
and then they’d have a catastrophic emotional
experience and they’d be all upset about

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:46.770
it crying away; which is of course what they
should do.

00:24:46.770 --> 00:24:50.539
Twenty minutes later it would wear off and
you’d say is there anything wrong with your

00:24:50.539 --> 00:24:54.320
left side and they’d say well yeah, I just
don’t feel like moving it.

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:59.740
So what seems to have happened is something
– so there’s a body representation center

00:24:59.740 --> 00:25:02.830
in the back of the right hemisphere.

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:06.890
You blow that sucker out with a stroke, and
that means that you can’t represent that

00:25:06.890 --> 00:25:08.790
part of your body anymore.

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:12.900
Maybe there are tatters of it left; it’s
like a network that’s damaged and under

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:17.110
some conditions you can more or less get it
to operate so you pour some cold water in

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:19.679
the ear and you shock the whole system.

00:25:19.679 --> 00:25:24.200
Then there’s enough neurological activity
so the network kicks in, even though it’s

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:28.980
damaged for ten minutes, and then you get
this tremendous burst of negative emotion

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:33.080
- which is what the right hemisphere should
be doing when you lose half of your body.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:37.409
You experience that when your left hemisphere
is flapping away trying to account for this

00:25:37.409 --> 00:25:41.520
– which it can’t, because it’s such
a massive, utter catastrophe.

00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:45.590
Then you know, the shock wears off and “bang!”

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:47.350
the system shuts back down, and “poof!”

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:48.930
you’re back in denial.

00:25:48.930 --> 00:25:52.971
That’s a good example of the relationship
between the left hemisphere and the right

00:25:52.971 --> 00:25:53.971
hemisphere.

00:25:53.971 --> 00:25:57.309
The left hemisphere has a fixed idea about
the world.

00:25:57.309 --> 00:26:02.830
So in some sense you might think about it
as the instantiation of your latest avatar;

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:05.799
you can certainly see that with people who
are ideologically possessed.

00:26:05.799 --> 00:26:10.940
They’ve got this verbal system that accounts
for the whole world and there’s just no

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:13.480
damn way that thing’s going anywhere.

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:19.520
It’s got this totalitarian aspect because
it wants to explain every last thing; it doesn’t

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:20.789
like anomaly at all.

00:26:20.789 --> 00:26:23.419
It hasn’t come to terms with anomaly at
all.

00:26:23.419 --> 00:26:28.320
There’s no sense of transformation within
the system, which is partly why totalitarian

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:31.440
systems die.

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:35.730
The Red Queen said to Alice in Wonderland
when she was in the underworld, “in my kingdom

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:39.059
you have to run as fast as you can just to
stay in the same place.”

00:26:39.059 --> 00:26:42.190
That’s why totalitarian systems don’t
work.

00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:45.230
The damn environment keeps shifting around
on them.

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:50.650
If they’re fixed in place, the gap between
their theory and reality gets larger and larger

00:26:50.650 --> 00:26:56.610
and larger and larger until finally they fall
into a pit; and that’s the end of it.

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:59.370
That’s what happens to people too, if they
don’t stay updated.

00:26:59.370 --> 00:27:07.020
They let things get away on them; eventually
the ignored reality comes flooding back, and

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:10.630
we’ll see that represented in mythology
because it’s represented very nicely in

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:11.630
mythology.

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:17.730
It’s why often dragons in fairytales, for
example, in the Saint George story, and in

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:21.750
the Hobbit for that matter – the dragon
is an eternal thing: it lives underneath the

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:25.309
earth and most of the time it leaves you the
hell alone.

00:27:25.309 --> 00:27:29.750
Now and then it comes back and it tries to
destroy the city, and then you have to zip

00:27:29.750 --> 00:27:32.950
out there and fight the thing so you can get
it to go back in its hole.

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:36.649
Maybe you get the treasure that it’s carrying,
and then it’s back in the hole for while;

00:27:36.649 --> 00:27:38.539
but that thing’s coming back.

00:27:38.539 --> 00:27:43.560
It’s always coming back, and so you build
the walls and all that, that’s fine.

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:46.640
A dragon can destroy any walls.

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:49.710
Walls aren’t enough – you have to be prepared
to go out there and fight the damn thing,

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:53.070
and that’s how you keep it at bay.

00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:56.501
That basically means you have to maintain
everything that you build, it’s something

00:27:56.501 --> 00:27:57.740
like that, right?

00:27:57.740 --> 00:28:01.200
Otherwise it falls apart with increasing entropy.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:12.030
Alright, so 
Goldberg thought about this as an anomaly

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.790
system, a novelty system and a routinization
system.

00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:24.049
I intended to think about it from more of
an Anthropological or an Ethological perspective;

00:28:24.049 --> 00:28:27.240
thinking about an animal (us) in our natural
environment.

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:33.340
It’s not so much novelty versus routinization,
as it’s explored versus unexplored territory.

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:38.539
That’s what the world’s made out of – it’s
made out of explored versus unexplored territory.

00:28:38.539 --> 00:28:43.919
So there’s a domain that you inhabit, which
is the center of the universe, from a mythological

00:28:43.919 --> 00:28:46.970
perspective, with you right in the middle
of it.

00:28:46.970 --> 00:28:51.650
Around you is this circle, which is your place,
which you move around with you, and in that

00:28:51.650 --> 00:28:53.809
circle you know what-the-hell’s going on.

00:28:53.809 --> 00:28:58.990
That’s partly because you’ve mapped it,
but it’s also partly because you don’t

00:28:58.990 --> 00:29:00.530
go outside of it.

00:29:00.530 --> 00:29:05.390
You have your friends and you have your routine
and that’s nested inside your culture, and

00:29:05.390 --> 00:29:08.380
you try and keep that thing from flipping
upside down.

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:14.159
You do that all sorts of way: partly by doing
the same thing all the time, but also partly

00:29:14.159 --> 00:29:19.200
by refusing to go anywhere that might seriously
challenge your beliefs.

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:22.929
Fair enough, you can understand that, although
it makes you weak to do that, because you’re

00:29:22.929 --> 00:29:24.660
not as prepared as you might be.

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:34.289
So you could say human beings are tribal,
social animals that have a territory.

00:29:34.289 --> 00:29:35.960
Then you think, “well what’s the territory?”

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:40.340
Well let’s say tree-dwelling chimp ancestors;
the territory’s the jungle.

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:41.340
No, it’s not.

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:43.789
That’s not the territory.

00:29:43.789 --> 00:29:51.039
The territory is a little bit of the jungle,
plus the things that come in from the outside,

00:29:51.039 --> 00:29:56.059
plus the primate dominance hierarchy that
exists in that area.

00:29:56.059 --> 00:30:00.440
So that’s the territory, that’s the environment.

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:05.610
For mythological reasons we tend to make a
separation between the environment and culture.

00:30:05.610 --> 00:30:11.529
Yes, it makes sense at one level of analysis,
but at another level of analysis all it does

00:30:11.529 --> 00:30:12.899
is screw you up.

00:30:12.899 --> 00:30:18.350
You’re territory, fundamentally, isn’t
the world; not the natural, objective world.

00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:21.520
You want to stay as far away from that thing
as you possibly can.

00:30:21.520 --> 00:30:28.260
Your territory is that circle of people with
whom you spend ninety percent of your time.

00:30:28.260 --> 00:30:33.000
If you’re a chimp, for example, or some
other kind of monkey, highly social, what

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:34.260
do you do all day?

00:30:34.260 --> 00:30:39.640
You sleep, you eat, you chew leaves if you’re
a chimp, and you groom other chimps.

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:45.140
It’s constant social interaction, and you’re
jockeying for a position in the dominance

00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:49.810
hierarchy like mean girls in that movie, you
know.

00:30:49.810 --> 00:30:53.000
You’re mapping out the social territory
knowing who’s who, and what’s what, and

00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:55.170
then trying to get up somewhere near the top.

00:30:55.170 --> 00:31:06.929
That’s your environment, that’s your environment,
and so your world is that social, natural

00:31:06.929 --> 00:31:12.610
circle, which is a four-dimensional territory,
because its got length, width and height – plus

00:31:12.610 --> 00:31:14.120
it stretches across time.

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:18.490
So it’s this transforming natural entity.

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:20.651
Inside that there’s a pyramid.

00:31:20.651 --> 00:31:24.130
The pyramid is made out of all the primates
that you interact with.

00:31:24.130 --> 00:31:27.590
That’s the world; circle with a pyramid
on it.

00:31:27.590 --> 00:31:31.240
Now I’ll give you a vision, this is a vision
I had once when I was trying to figure this

00:31:31.240 --> 00:31:32.240
stuff out.

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:33.990
Now what is the world?

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:40.490
So imagine that there’s a field in front
of you that stretches as far as you can see.

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:43.120
You’re flying above it.

00:31:43.120 --> 00:31:45.580
What you see everywhere are these pyramids.

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:50.039
Let’s say for the sake of argument, that
they’re skyscrapers made out of glass.

00:31:50.039 --> 00:31:51.990
There are tears in them.

00:31:51.990 --> 00:31:56.510
You fly down and one’s here and one’s
here, and some of them are really big and

00:31:56.510 --> 00:31:58.549
some of them are really small.

00:31:58.549 --> 00:32:00.010
They’re just everywhere.

00:32:00.010 --> 00:32:03.910
You’re flying around above them, and you
fly close enough to look inside and what you

00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:08.780
see inside them are people all packed together,
trying to scramble up to the top.

00:32:08.780 --> 00:32:12.210
That’s the world, that’s reality.

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:16.110
The funny thing is, is that you can be in
one of those pyramids clambering away to the

00:32:16.110 --> 00:32:19.289
top, or you can be flying above it – those
are your options.

00:32:19.289 --> 00:32:23.120
If you have to choose, you should be flying
above it.

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:28.789
So the relationship between the self and the
ego avatar is the same as the relationship

00:32:28.789 --> 00:32:32.840
between the thing that’s flying above, and
the thing that’s within one of those pyramids

00:32:32.840 --> 00:32:35.070
trying to scramble to the top.

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:36.940
You know, well you say well I’m going to
get to the top.

00:32:36.940 --> 00:32:38.649
“Top of what?”

00:32:38.649 --> 00:32:41.830
There’s a hundred tops, there’s two hundred
tops.

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:44.779
Then you might say, “well is there anything
above the top?”

00:32:44.779 --> 00:32:51.429
There is, there’s the part of you that isn’t
entrapped within that particular system and

00:32:51.429 --> 00:32:53.470
that can move from place to place.

00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:57.200
If you look at Egyptian mythology: so you
know you’ll see if you look at the back

00:32:57.200 --> 00:33:00.340
of the American dollar bill, that’s a symbol
of value, right?

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:02.600
Okay, so what’s the pyramid?

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:04.540
There’s a pyramid on it, right?

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:06.019
What’s the top of a pyramid?

00:33:06.019 --> 00:33:08.179
It’s a triangle, it’s separate from the
pyramid.

00:33:08.179 --> 00:33:09.909
There’s an eye inside of it.

00:33:09.909 --> 00:33:15.100
That’s because the top of the pyramid is
an eye, and it’s separate from the pyramid.

00:33:15.100 --> 00:33:23.500
What that means, fundamentally, is that the
thing that transcends the structure is attention,

00:33:23.500 --> 00:33:25.250
voluntary attention.

00:33:25.250 --> 00:33:31.140
Now the Egyptians worshiped voluntary attention
in the form of Horus, because he was a God.

00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:33.730
I’ll tell you about Horus in detail.

00:33:33.730 --> 00:33:38.289
One of Horus’ avatars was the falcon.

00:33:38.289 --> 00:33:40.559
The reason for that is falcons can really,
really, really see.

00:33:40.559 --> 00:33:45.110
They fly around, and so they’re like the
king’s eyes.

00:33:45.110 --> 00:33:48.220
Like the little bird Zazu in the Lion King.

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:51.950
He was the king’s eyes, the thing that flies
around above.

00:33:51.950 --> 00:33:56.570
He was a particular enemy of Scar, remember
Scar eats him at the beginning and then spits

00:33:56.570 --> 00:33:57.680
him out.

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:01.789
The reason that the King’s eyes is an enemy
of scar is because the King’s eyes can see

00:34:01.789 --> 00:34:03.340
what he is, right?

00:34:03.340 --> 00:34:08.599
He’s the eternal force that works to disrupt
the kingdom and bring everything into ruins.

00:34:08.599 --> 00:34:14.429
Classic myth, classic myth, partly accounting
for why it was the largest selling, highest

00:34:14.429 --> 00:34:16.000
grossing cartoon in Disney history.

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:22.280
They got the mythology pretty right.

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:34.879
So you might say, “what’s a human being
like?”

00:34:34.879 --> 00:34:42.880
Remember I told you that when Penfield was
mapping the brain he was poking people.

00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:47.510
You can either poke them along the sensory
strip, which is – if the motor strip is

00:34:47.510 --> 00:34:51.139
here, the sensory strip is just behind it.

00:34:51.139 --> 00:34:56.030
The motor strip is where the motor systems
of your body are represented in so far as

00:34:56.030 --> 00:34:57.970
you can control them.

00:34:57.970 --> 00:35:03.190
The sensory strip is where the sensory systems
are represented in so far as you can feel

00:35:03.190 --> 00:35:04.190
them.

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:10.650
So what this is, is a – the body is laid
out weirdly on the motor cortex.

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:14.680
One of the things that’s pretty funny is
that the genitalia area in the sensory cortex

00:35:14.680 --> 00:35:16.800
is right beside the feet.

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:22.320
So I think that’s why people have foot fetishes
and love foot massages, is because they’re

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:27.000
spreading activation from the foot area to
the genital area, and God only knows why that

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:28.000
is.

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:32.060
Maybe because you had to have had really sensitive
feet to stomp around without stepping on really

00:35:32.060 --> 00:35:33.670
horrible things.

00:35:33.670 --> 00:35:41.190
So you can put this weird, stretched-out,
body representation together in a representational

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:46.250
form like this, they call that the homunculus,
which is like a little-man that exists inside

00:35:46.250 --> 00:35:47.280
your brain.

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:50.310
So what you see, that’s the motor-homunculus,
not the sensory-homunculus.

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:52.630
So what do you see?

00:35:52.630 --> 00:35:55.260
Well what is a person as far as its brain
is concerned?

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:58.420
Well, hand – that’s for sure.

00:35:58.420 --> 00:36:03.250
Thumb, that’s for sure – your thumb is
as representative of your whole body.

00:36:03.250 --> 00:36:09.500
It’s because your torso is hardly represented
at all.

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:10.700
Why is that?

00:36:10.700 --> 00:36:14.070
How many things do you manipulate with your
chest?

00:36:14.070 --> 00:36:15.430
None.

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:19.589
You don’t have motor control over it, so
it’s not represented.

00:36:19.589 --> 00:36:23.580
So, the hands man, those things, they’re
represented like mad.

00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:26.340
So are the face, and the lips and the tongue.

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:30.589
There’s a bunch of reasons for that: One
is when you’re an infant, you explore like

00:36:30.589 --> 00:36:35.330
mad with your mouth – but you do that your
whole life because when you eat- you know

00:36:35.330 --> 00:36:36.330
what it’s like.

00:36:36.330 --> 00:36:40.410
If you’re eating something and there’s
a bit of sand in it, you find it and you take

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:41.410
it out.

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:45.170
You can detect this teeny, little thing that’s
not supposed to be in your mouth.

00:36:45.170 --> 00:36:48.970
Well that’s a good thing because lots of
things aren’t supposed to be in your mouth.

00:36:48.970 --> 00:36:50.680
That’s part of the reason.

00:36:50.680 --> 00:36:54.220
The other part of the reason is, well think
about what you do with your tongue?

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:58.660
There are a lot of things you do with your
tongue: Some of them are eating, some of them

00:36:58.660 --> 00:36:59.880
are talking.

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:04.800
Some of them are all sorts of intimate behaviors,
that thing is wired up; and it’s wired up

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:06.130
right at birth.

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:12.140
Then it’s the same with your lips and the
rest of your mouth, you use that to communicate.

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:16.270
Your hands wander around, taking the world
apart, and putting it back together, which

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:20.910
is exactly what your brain does, and then
you blab about what you’re doing to everyone.

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:23.160
That’s basically a human being.

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:29.480
I like to think of this thing as it’s a
comical representation of the hero that goes

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:31.800
out to confront the world, right?

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:33.270
The hero has two elements.

00:37:33.270 --> 00:37:39.700
One is explore, and that’s where the west
is powerful.

00:37:39.700 --> 00:37:43.450
We have a really well developed mythology
of the exploratory hero.

00:37:43.450 --> 00:37:46.130
Where we’re not so well developed is the
second part.

00:37:46.130 --> 00:37:51.310
In the second part of the classic hero story,
you’re supposed to come back and communicate

00:37:51.310 --> 00:37:53.349
about what you’ve found.

00:37:53.349 --> 00:37:57.609
So if there’s an individual exploration
element, and then there’s a communicate

00:37:57.609 --> 00:38:00.160
and update the culture element.

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:04.360
Both of those are equally important an it’s
something we don’t do well, we don’t have

00:38:04.360 --> 00:38:09.530
much respect for tradition in the west, and
it’s a really, really big problem.

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:14.530
The ethical responsibility of a human being
is to take the dead culture, so that’s the

00:38:14.530 --> 00:38:20.200
dead father, or the dead God, and to revivify
it with attention and communication.

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:23.730
If you fail to do that then everything disappears.

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:27.530
We’re failing to do that – we’re leaving
it all behind instead.

00:38:27.530 --> 00:38:29.579
It’s not a good idea.

00:38:29.579 --> 00:38:36.280
You know if the old Gods die as Nietzsche
said they had, new Gods swoop in very rapidly

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:41.630
and if you thought the old God was bad, you
wait until the new God gets a hold of you.

00:38:41.630 --> 00:38:46.750
So, well we saw what happened in the twentieth
century, with all of the ideologies that sprang

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:49.310
up in the absence of classical religious belief.

00:38:49.310 --> 00:38:54.710
Okay, you’re not so thrilled with Catholicism,
if you think about that as the fundamental

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:56.170
tradition of the West.

00:38:56.170 --> 00:38:58.630
You know, with the witch burnings and the
inquisition and all that.

00:38:58.630 --> 00:39:03.450
It’s like yeah, yeah, get a load of the
Nazis and then see what you think; or the

00:39:03.450 --> 00:39:11.910
Communists for that matter - from bad to worse
in an unimaginable way.

00:39:11.910 --> 00:39:17.619
You know, and it’s partly because those
archaic systems, those archaic, religious

00:39:17.619 --> 00:39:21.810
systems, they weren’t ideologies, they were
a whole different class of thing.

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:25.859
We don’t understand them very well, and
it’s not good because you cannot live without

00:39:25.859 --> 00:39:26.920
understanding them.

00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:28.700
You don’t know who you are.

00:39:28.700 --> 00:39:32.599
Then you fall prey to weird mental diseases,
like Nihilism, that’s one.

00:39:32.599 --> 00:39:37.869
The other one is ideological possession – so
you become a Totalitarian or a Nihilist.

00:39:37.869 --> 00:39:40.170
That’s a bad outcome.

00:39:40.170 --> 00:39:42.190
You might think, “well, what’s the alternative?”

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:44.690
Well, you know what happens in Pinocchio,
right?

00:39:44.690 --> 00:39:45.710
You’ve watched Pinocchio?

00:39:45.710 --> 00:39:48.960
Okay, I often show Pinocchio to this class,
but I don’t know if I’m going to do that

00:39:48.960 --> 00:39:49.960
this year.

00:39:49.960 --> 00:39:52.670
Anyways, Pinocchio, he’s a puppet, a marionette.

00:39:52.670 --> 00:39:53.960
Someone else is pulling his strings.

00:39:53.960 --> 00:39:55.370
He has a wooden head.

00:39:55.370 --> 00:39:56.390
He’s not very bright.

00:39:56.390 --> 00:39:58.320
He hasn’t banged the world at all.

00:39:58.320 --> 00:40:02.420
He’s got a good father, the old Geppetto,
who would really rather that he was a real

00:40:02.420 --> 00:40:04.120
boy, a real person.

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:09.060
So he’s the kind of father that wants to
produce an individual, not a slave.

00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:13.339
That’s why in his workshop it’s all friendly
and there’s a nice fire, and he’s making

00:40:13.339 --> 00:40:16.770
toys and there’s lots of music, it’s like
“Go Geppetto!” he’s a good guy.

00:40:16.770 --> 00:40:19.210
He makes this little puppet and he sends him
out in the world.

00:40:19.210 --> 00:40:20.819
He says, “go be a real boy.”

00:40:20.819 --> 00:40:28.970
Well, Pinocchio runs into a whole class of
different obstacles; one is evil, that’s

00:40:28.970 --> 00:40:36.099
the Coachman, and the Italian stereotype guy
who makes him a dancing puppet celebrity.

00:40:36.099 --> 00:40:41.410
I think that’s pretty funny, that’s really
prescient.

00:40:41.410 --> 00:40:47.460
You might think well what’s the goal of
your typical bone-headed, mid-adolescent in

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:49.480
North America – unearned celebrity.

00:40:49.480 --> 00:40:52.990
Well that’s exactly what Pinocchio goes
for first.

00:40:52.990 --> 00:40:57.140
That doesn’t work out so well because he
ends up a slave and then the next thing that

00:40:57.140 --> 00:41:01.730
happens is he capitalizes on the fact that
he’s vulnerable, and pretends he’s sick

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:05.970
and goes off for a vacation, under the direction
of the Coachman (who’s basically Satan in

00:41:05.970 --> 00:41:06.970
disguise).

00:41:06.970 --> 00:41:12.690
Then he goes out to treasure island and meets
all of the delinquents who are just like Nazis,

00:41:12.690 --> 00:41:17.920
and turns out in fact the whole ploy is to
enslave everyone, to deprive them of their

00:41:17.920 --> 00:41:21.990
voice – that’s the donkey’s breying
– and to make them into slaves.

00:41:21.990 --> 00:41:28.680
Pretty damn horrifying and that movie came
out around the time that the Nazis were really

00:41:28.680 --> 00:41:31.320
grasping power in central Europe.

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:33.210
They got it exactly right.

00:41:33.210 --> 00:41:36.619
Here’s what’s really going on behind the
scenes.

00:41:36.619 --> 00:41:40.280
Then what happens is Pinocchio turns halfway
into a braying donkey.

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:44.690
He’s gone off the path and in order to get
back on it he tries to go home.

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:48.540
It’s too late, he can’t go home, because
he’s not a kid anymore.

00:41:48.540 --> 00:41:52.250
He goes home and Dad isn’t there, surprise,
surprise.

00:41:52.250 --> 00:41:58.010
Once you grow up and you go home, Dad isn’t
there – it’s just another human being.

00:41:58.010 --> 00:42:00.560
So then what happens is he has to go into
the ocean.

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:02.510
Well that’s particularly weird, right?

00:42:02.510 --> 00:42:07.839
There he is, half donkey, he’s a puppet,
the dove comes down and drops him a message

00:42:07.839 --> 00:42:08.880
from God.

00:42:08.880 --> 00:42:13.819
It’s like the Holy Spirit descending, except
it’s from the blue fairy, weirdly enough.

00:42:13.819 --> 00:42:18.280
The message says you have to go to the deepest
part of the ocean and rescue your father.

00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:23.580
Yeah that is what you have to do if you don’t
want to be a bloody, puppet donkey.

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:28.020
You have to go down into the depths and find
your dead Father and you have to revivify

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:29.020
him.

00:42:29.020 --> 00:42:30.020
That’s what happens.

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:34.349
So Pinocchio goes way down there into the
deepest depths and finds Monstro, who’s

00:42:34.349 --> 00:42:38.990
a monster so terrible that the fish swim away
at the sound of his voice.

00:42:38.990 --> 00:42:45.410
Just like, “he who shall not be named – Voldemort.”

00:42:45.410 --> 00:42:47.599
You can’t even say his damn name.

00:42:47.599 --> 00:42:49.109
Same with Monstro.

00:42:49.109 --> 00:42:53.320
Pinocchio goes down there, he goes into the
whale, what happens?

00:42:53.320 --> 00:42:57.490
Geppetto says there’s no getting out of
this, there’s no getting out of this.

00:42:57.490 --> 00:43:01.050
We just have to sit here and cook fish and
Pinocchio thinks, “cook!”

00:43:01.050 --> 00:43:05.520
He starts breaking up all the chairs and everything
and his Father is freaking out, “what are

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:06.880
you doing breaking all of these chairs?

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:08.050
What are we going to sit out?”

00:43:08.050 --> 00:43:09.619
Pinocchio lights them on fire.

00:43:09.619 --> 00:43:11.040
He’s a master of fire.

00:43:11.040 --> 00:43:12.760
It’s a Shemanic ritual.

00:43:12.760 --> 00:43:17.420
He is a master of fire and that’s what human
being is.

00:43:17.420 --> 00:43:21.630
He gets everything burning, of course Geppetto’s
all freaked out about that because what are

00:43:21.630 --> 00:43:24.230
the going to sit on, and Pinocchio thinks,
“who cares about sitting?

00:43:24.230 --> 00:43:27.650
We’re going to solve the problem once and
for all.”

00:43:27.650 --> 00:43:31.140
Then the whale turns into a fire-breathing
dragon, which is pretty damn weird thing for

00:43:31.140 --> 00:43:32.260
a whale to do.

00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:36.590
He half-kills Pinocchio, he washes up on shore
dead, but he saves his father.

00:43:36.590 --> 00:43:40.910
Then at the very end of the movie he’s lying
on his bed, all drowned.

00:43:40.910 --> 00:43:43.640
So he’s a dead puppet now.

00:43:43.640 --> 00:43:46.280
Do you want to be a live puppet or a dead
puppet?

00:43:46.280 --> 00:43:50.150
Well the maybe the dead puppet is better than
the live puppet, it depends on where your

00:43:50.150 --> 00:43:51.150
going.

00:43:51.150 --> 00:43:54.970
Then he’s on the bed, and the blue fairy
comes down and says well you’ve been honest

00:43:54.970 --> 00:43:56.089
and courageous, “bang!”

00:43:56.089 --> 00:43:57.089
now you’re real.

00:43:57.089 --> 00:44:04.730
Then they have a little part, and that’s
just exactly right, they got that exactly

00:44:04.730 --> 00:44:05.730
right.

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:09.210
That’s part of the hero mythology, it’s
the rescuing of the father.

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:14.180
Student: Why do you have to rescue the father?

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:16.230
Otherwise you’re a corpse.

00:44:16.230 --> 00:44:23.140
You’re a historical creature, you’re a
cultural creature.

00:44:23.140 --> 00:44:26.440
You cannot live without your culture.

00:44:26.440 --> 00:44:28.670
You think you can, but you can’t.

00:44:28.670 --> 00:44:32.860
it disappears, and look, I’ll give an example.

00:44:32.860 --> 00:44:36.780
In the Mesopotamian creation myth, which I’m
going to tell you about next week, one of

00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:42.000
the things that happens is that the first
round of Gods, so they’re like primordial

00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:46.060
forces, they’re pretty damn careless, and
they kill their father.

00:44:46.060 --> 00:44:49.500
They make this place to live on his corpse.

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:53.880
That’s where we live, we live on the corpse
of our ancestors.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:57.460
We live in the society, it’s dead, it’s
the product of dead people.

00:44:57.460 --> 00:45:00.300
It’s got this dead element, but we’re
living on it.

00:45:00.300 --> 00:45:05.650
They’re very careless, they kill it, so
they render it completely dead.

00:45:05.650 --> 00:45:10.400
Well what happens is Tiamat, who’s the Mother
of Chaos, she comes flooding back and she

00:45:10.400 --> 00:45:14.150
thinks, “oh you killed the thing that protects
you.

00:45:14.150 --> 00:45:16.030
You just wait to see what happens.”

00:45:16.030 --> 00:45:20.510
That’s exactly it, if you kill the thing
that structures you, you won’t know it – it’s

00:45:20.510 --> 00:45:23.540
like Nietzsche’s prediction from the death
of God.

00:45:23.540 --> 00:45:28.160
You killed this, think it doesn’t make any
difference, - wrong.

00:45:28.160 --> 00:45:31.560
It’s like the Striders protecting the Hobbits.

00:45:31.560 --> 00:45:36.099
If you get rid of the thing that protects
you then chaos comes flooding in and you have

00:45:36.099 --> 00:45:37.970
no idea what’s possible.

00:45:37.970 --> 00:45:40.460
We do, though, because we lived through the
twentieth century.

00:45:40.460 --> 00:45:42.680
We know exactly what’s possible.

00:45:42.680 --> 00:45:46.560
Hundreds of millions of unnecessary deaths,
that’s what’s possible.

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:52.160
We’re lucky we didn’t blow the whole damn
thing up, and we still could.

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:56.490
People pursing their dead ideologies – you
don’t rescue the dead Father, than you’re

00:45:56.490 --> 00:45:58.840
the puppet of death, that’s what you are.

00:45:58.840 --> 00:46:02.910
Okay, on that cheery note.

